WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.740
From victim blaming to downplaying a&nbsp;
serious disorder, these ‘90s shows are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:08.796
proof that trauma can’t be fixed with a&nbsp;
heart to heart and a half hour of comedy.

00:00:08.796 --> 00:00:12.840
The sixth episode of Seinfeld season&nbsp;
9, called "The Merv Griffin Show,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:16.680
finds one funny subplot counteracted&nbsp;
by the distastefulness of another.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:21.240
When Kramer discovers the discarded set from&nbsp;
Griffin's long-running talk show in the trash,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:27.060
he brings it home and turns his apartment into a&nbsp;
temporary studio. The gags that result are good,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:31.680
as Kramer interviews his friends and cracks&nbsp;
jokes. The rest of the episode, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:36.120
centers around Jerry and his girlfriend&nbsp;
Celia, who has a priceless collection of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:40.920
vintage toys in her living room. Their storyline&nbsp;
quickly takes a dark and uncomfortable turn.

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:43.260
When they return to her apartment after a date,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:47.520
Jerry drools over her Superball and&nbsp;
original G.I. Joe action figure with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:52.140
the frogman suit. Celia won't let him touch&nbsp;
the collection and asks for a pain reliever.

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:59.940
"will not cause drowsiness…. MAY CAUSE drowsiness"

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:05.700
Jerry purposely gives her a drowsy&nbsp;
tablet so he can play with the toys&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:09.360
while she sleeps on the couch. George&nbsp;
and Elaine eventually want in on it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:14.760
so they treat Celia to a turkey and red wine&nbsp;
dinner, hoping the combination of tryptophan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:18.360
alcohol, and a dull home movie&nbsp;
marathon will knock her out.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:22.800
The bright spot of the episode comes when&nbsp;
Kramer is horrified by Jerry's actions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:28.320
but the entire runtime is plagued by allusions&nbsp;
to date rape and sexual assault. Shots of Celia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:34.020
unconscious and sprawled across her couch fell&nbsp;
flat then and still do now. The joke is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:39.000
Jerry is drugging her to take advantage of her&nbsp;
toy collection, not her, but there's no punchline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:44.820
here. The bit is in incredibly poor taste, and&nbsp;
casts an unfortunate shadow on the show's legacy.

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:50.340
Dawson's Creek had a lot of dangerous,&nbsp;
misogynistic attitudes toward women and sex&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:55.020
throughout its six seasons, but no character&nbsp;
suffered more than Jen. Not only does she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:59.340
suffer a lame, offscreen death, Jen's motive&nbsp;
throughout the show was, in her own words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:04.620
to allow Dawson to figure out who he really loved.&nbsp;
The writers tried their hardest to portray her as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:09.180
needy, promiscuous and one-dimensional —&nbsp;
which opened her up to unnecessary peril,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:13.200
like the time she and Pacey wind up&nbsp;
stranded in the ocean during a huge storm.

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:18.360
But Jen's lowest moment on the show came&nbsp;
in Season 2, when she was victim-shamed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:23.280
by her own family. In "Full Moon Rising,"&nbsp;
Vincent tries taking things too far with Jen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:28.980
who objects to his advances. When her grandmother&nbsp;
gets home before things can get worse, rather than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:35.052
offering affirmation or support, she chastises&nbsp;
and shames Jen for her promiscuous behavior.

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:40.740
"I will not allow you to slide back into&nbsp;
your reprehensible New York behavior,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:43.464
not while you're under my care."

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:47.640
Jen being humiliated and blamed by&nbsp;
a loved one punctuates the show's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:52.680
hotbed of misconceptions about sex and rape&nbsp;
culture, and perpetuates victim silence.

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:57.480
When Dawson's Creek arrived on Netflix, longtime&nbsp;
fans began changing their opinions of Jen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:01.980
finally seeing her as a character with poise&nbsp;
and grace. It's a mindset they should have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:06.180
had 20 years ago. Though Jen's story has&nbsp;
aged better than some others on the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:12.168
the victim-shaming scene in Season 2 was bad when&nbsp;
it aired and is even worse through a modern lens.

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:18.180
In the third season of Full House, middle child&nbsp;
Stephanie developed PTSD after an earthquake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:23.280
and was "cured" within minutes of speaking&nbsp;
to a child psychologist. In the next season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:27.768
her older sister D.J. develops an eating&nbsp;
disorder, which is handled similarly.

00:03:28.488 --> 00:03:33.576
"There is no way I'm wearing a swimsuit in front&nbsp;
of everyone until I look like one of these models"

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:40.080
The portrayal of D.J.'s anorexia stigmatizes&nbsp;
serious conditions, employing harmful stereotypes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:45.540
of people being overly dramatic, uninterested in&nbsp;
getting help and immediately curable.The episode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:51.420
does handle anorexia gently and empathetically: no&nbsp;
character shames D.J. for her actions, and they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:56.340
all genuinely concerned for her. But the show's&nbsp;
short, open-and-shut structure doesn't line up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.340 --> 00:04:01.860
with the disorder's months or years-long effects&nbsp;
in real life. D.J.'s onscreen anorexia is hard to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:07.500
watch, especially since actor Candace Cameron Bure&nbsp;
later revealed she developed an eating disorder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:12.180
after the show ended, but the "inspirational&nbsp;
lecture from dad" bit at the end of the episode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:17.748
undercuts the progress that people with eating&nbsp;
disorders are continuously striving to make.

